.TH ALSP 4
.SH NAME
alsp, Def, Type, Refs, Impls, Doc, Comp, Syms, Diag, Fmt \- acme's windows in a language server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B alsp
[
.B -D
] [
.B -m
.I mtpt
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
]
.PP
.B Def
.PP
.B Type
.PP
.B Refs
.PP
.B Impls
.PP
.B Doc
.PP
.B Comp
.PP
.B Syms
.PP
.B Diag
.PP
.B Fmt
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Alsp
puts the text of
.IR acme (1)'s
windows in
.IR lspfs (4),
and the tokens and diagnostics
.I lspfs
gives back in each window's
.IR astyles (4)
layers.
It posts itself as
.BI /srv/ srvname
.RB ( /srv/alsp )
and mounts itself on
.I mtpt
.RB ( /mnt/alsp ),
then waits for
.B /srv/acme
and follows its windows.
.B Acmestart
starts it after
.I astyles
and
.IR lspfs ,
before acme.
.PP
A window whose file the first
.B file=
of the rules in
.B $home/lib/lsp
or
.B /lib/lsp
matches is opened in
.IR lspfs ,
and each edit to it is sent there, as acme's log gives it.
The edits are counted, as acme counts them, so each thing
.I alsp
reads or writes is at a known place in the window's history:
the text of an insert too long for acme's log is read under the edit's
.B seq
guard (see
.IR astyles (4)),
and if the window has moved on,
.I alsp
reads the whole body again and sends it.
.I Get
sends the body again.
Tokens go in the window's layer
.BR lsp ,
diagnostics in
.BR diagnostics ,
each written under the guard of the last edit sent, and again when it was refused for a newer one.
.PP
After a
.IR Put ,
.I alsp
does what the rule's
.B put=
says:
.L fmt
lays the window out, by
.IR lspfs 's
.BR fmt ,
as one change, so one
.I Undo
takes it back; the window is left dirty.
A format of text that changed meanwhile is not applied.
Writing
.BI fmt " winid"
to
.B ctl
does the same at once.
.PP
The commands work on the window they are run in, at dot:
.I Def
and
.I Type
plumb the definition of the word or of its type;
.I Refs
and
.I Impls
list the places the word is used and implemented, each with its function, by
.IR func (1);
.I Doc
gives the diagnostics on the line, then what the server says of the word;
.I Comp
lists the completions at dot;
.I Syms
lists the file's symbols;
.I Diag
lists its diagnostics;
and
.I Fmt
lays it out.
The lists are lines that button 3 opens.
.SH FILES
.TF /mnt/alsp/ctl
.TP
.B /mnt/alsp/ctl
.BI fmt " winid"
.PD
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/alsp.c
.br
.B /acme/bin/Def
and the others, on
.B /acme/bin/lspq
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (4),
.IR astyles (4),
.IR lspfs (4)
.SH BUGS
A zeroxed window is not followed; its file's first window is.
